# PHASE 7 — ACCESSIBILITY AND PERFORMANCE

The animated hero must remain lightweight and accessible.

## Image loading

- Load the prominent above-the-fold assets appropriately.
- Use lazy loading for non-visible secondary assets where suitable.
- Preserve explicit image dimensions or aspect ratios to prevent layout shifts.
- Use asynchronous image decoding where supported.
- Avoid loading duplicate copies of the same asset.
- Avoid unnecessarily high-resolution decoding for tiny background cards.
- Do not modify the supplied URLs or add unverified transformations.

## Rendering performance

Prefer CSS transforms and opacity for animation.

Avoid expensive filters, excessive repaints, heavy blur animations, or continuous JavaScript-driven layout calculations.

Reuse existing animation libraries if the project already has them.

Avoid creating dozens of independently expensive animation loops.

If using shared animation logic or CSS keyframes, keep it maintainable and appropriately scoped.

## Accessibility

- Respect `prefers-reduced-motion`.
- Disable or substantially reduce continuous movement when reduced motion is requested.
- Ensure the main hero copy stays readable throughout every animation state.
- Preserve keyboard navigation and existing button accessibility.
- Keep authentication buttons fully interactive during animations.
- Use descriptive image alternatives if the images communicate meaningful content. If an image card is purely decorative, use an appropriate empty alternative text to avoid repetitive screen-reader output.
- Do not hide essential content behind animation or hover interactions.

The page should remain complete and understandable even when all animations are disabled.

